{"causes": ["The Punic Wars were sparked by a long-standing rivalry between Rome and Carthage, fueled by competition for control of the Mediterranean trade routes and resources.", "Hannibal's invasion of Italy was a direct response to the Roman declaration of war on Carthage.", "The Second Punic War was also influenced by the broader dynamics of the ancient Mediterranean world, including the rise of the Hellenistic kingdoms and the decline of the Achaemenid Empire."], "effects": ["The Second Punic War led to the defeat of Carthage and the rise of Rome as a dominant power in the Mediterranean world.", "The war's outcome contributed to the eventual downfall of the Hellenistic kingdoms and the transformation of the Roman Republic into a major world power.", "The Second Punic War also had significant cultural and economic implications, including the spread of Roman culture and the development of new trade routes."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "hannibal", "sourceName": "Hannibal", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "second-punic-war", "targetName": "The Second Punic War", "context": "Hannibal led the Carthaginian forces during the Second Punic War, famously crossing the Alps with his elephants in 218 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "scipio-africanus", "sourceName": "Scipio Africanus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "second-punic-war", "targetName": "The Second Punic War", "context": "Scipio Africanus led the Roman forces to victory over Hannibal at the Battle of Zama in 202 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "roman-republic", "sourceName": "The Roman Republic", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "second-punic-war", "targetName": "The Second Punic War", "context": "The Second Punic War was fought within the context of the Roman Republic's expansion and growth."}, {"sourceSlug": "carthage", "sourceName": "Carthage", "verb": "FRAMES", "targetSlug": "second-punic-war", "targetName": "The Second Punic War", "context": "The Second Punic War was a defining moment in the history of Carthage, marking the beginning of the end of the city's power and influence."}, {"sourceSlug": "mediterranean-sea", "sourceName": "The Mediterranean Sea", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "second-punic-war", "targetName": "The Second Punic War", "context": "The Second Punic War took place in the Mediterranean Sea, with key battles fought in modern-day Spain, France, and Italy."}], "places": [{"name": "Cannae, Italy", "role": "Major battle"}, {"name": "Zama, Tunisia", "role": "Decisive battle"}, {"name": "Carthage, Tunisia", "role": "Capital city"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Second Punic War", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "second-punic-war"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6271"], "tags": ["war", "Ancient & Classical Wars", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Second Punic War left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 75 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Reshaped political boundaries and power dynamics. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Second Punic War had a profound impact on the course of world history, contributing to the rise of Rome as a dominant power in the Mediterranean world and shaping the development of Western civilization.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-07T06:14:20Z", "editorId": "Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct·cloud·GH#27084605516", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Second war between the roman republic and carthage, fought between 218 and 201 bce Took place in Mediterranean Sea. Date: 217 BCE. Ended: 200 BCE.", "newValue": "The Second Punic War was a pivotal conflict between the Roman Republic and Carthage, fought from 218 to 201 BCE, that significantly altered the Mediterranean balance of power and shaped the course of Roman expansion. This global conflict, centered in the Mediterranean Sea, saw the Roman general Hannibal clash with Roman forces under the leadership of Scipio Africanus, ultimately resulting in the defeat of Carthage and the rise of Rome as a dominant power in the region. The war's outcome had far-reaching consequences, contributing to the eventual downfall of Carthage and the transformation of the Roman Republic into a major world power. The legacy of the Second Punic War can be seen in the subsequent Roman expansion and the decline of the Hellenistic kingdoms.

The Second Punic War was a defining moment in the ancient Mediterranean world, marked by Hannibal's famous crossing of the Alps and the titanic struggles between Roman and Carthaginian forces. The conflict saw the deployment of novel military tactics, including the use of war elephants, and the emergence of key figures such as Hannibal and Scipio Africanus. The war's outcome had significant implications for the future of the Roman Republic and the Mediterranean world, setting the stage for the rise of Rome and the decline of Carthage.

